<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Christian Social Party (Austria)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1891">1891</a>–<a href="/years/1934">1934</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Christian Social Party was an Austrian Catholic conservative party founded in 1891 and led in its early years by Karl Lueger, mayor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>. It was strongest among the peasantry, the clergy and the lower middle class, and after 1920 it dominated the governments of the First Austrian Republic under leaders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ignaz-seipel">Ignaz Seipel</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/engelbert-dollfuss">Engelbert Dollfuss</a>. In 1934 it was absorbed into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatherland-front-austria">Fatherland Front</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-peoples-party">Austrian People's Party</a> succeeded it in 1945.</p></div>
